#usda 1.0
(
    defaultPrim = "HydrogenSupplySystem"
    metersPerUnit = 1.0
    upAxis = "Z"
    doc = """The hydrogen supply cabinet, made solid. Spawn this file, not
hydrogen_supply_system.usd.

The imported file carries no collision of any kind, measured rather than assumed:
/World/node_/mesh_ has MaterialBindingAPI and nothing else -- no PhysicsCollisionAPI, no
PhysicsMeshCollisionAPI, no RigidBodyAPI, and no prim anywhere in the file has them. Spawning
it as it comes therefore gives a cabinet the robot drives straight through.

Wrapping rather than editing is what keeps the import honest. The reference below brings the
original in unchanged, so every number that describes it still describes the file copied out
of openflex_ws -- 11,503,617 points, 3,834,539 faces, and the metresPerUnit = 0.01 that the
spawn's 0.001 scale exists to compensate for (see
isaac_sim_arena.constants.HYDROGEN_SUPPLY_SYSTEM_SCALE). Editing the original would have
meant rewriting 276 MB to add two lines, and would have invalidated the md5 the copy is
recorded under.

The collider is authored on the mesh itself and left to PhysX to size, rather than typed in
as a box. physics:approximation = "boundingCube" makes the collider the mesh's own
axis-aligned bounds, which for a rectangular cabinet is the cabinet, so there are no second
set of dimensions to drift out of step with the visual. It is also the only affordable
approximation here: 3.8 M faces rules out a triangle mesh or a convex decomposition, and
"boundingCube" costs a bounds computation the renderer has already done.

The result is a *static* collider -- a collider with no rigid body, which is the same thing
the ground plane is. PhysX uses those to block dynamic bodies, so the robot and its arms stop
at the cabinet's surface while the cabinet itself never moves or falls.
"""
)

def Xform "HydrogenSupplySystem" (
    kind = "component"
)
{
    def Xform "cabinet" (
        references = @./hydrogen_supply_system.usd@</World>
    )
    {
        # The reference is to /World, so its own two children arrive here: node_ holding the
        # single merged mesh, and Looks holding the material bound to it. The override below
        # reaches the mesh through the reference; nothing in this file is instanceable, so
        # the override is legal -- checked on the source stage, where shelf, table, rack and
        # every ancestor of the mesh report instanceable = False.
        over "node_"
        {
            over "mesh_" (
                prepend apiSchemas = ["PhysicsCollisionAPI", "PhysicsMeshCollisionAPI"]
            )
            {
                uniform token physics:approximation = "boundingCube"
            }
        }
    }
}
